    "abstract": "Historic landscapes are frequently capitalized into accommodation prices, yet existing valuation frameworks still rely heavily on planar proximity or coarse visibility proxies that fail to capture room-level three-dimensional exposure in dense urban settings. This study develops an automated multi scalar analytical framework that integrates room level three dimensional viewshed modelling, street-view image perception, semantic segmentation, and explainable machine learning to estimate how multidimensional visual exposure translates into hotel room premiums in Beijing’s historic core. The analysis yields four main findings. First, three-dimensional exposure variables, especially maximum visual depth, provide substantially greater explanatory power than conventional visible-area measures, showing that being spatially close is not equivalent to seeing deeply. Second, room premiums respond to visual environments through nonlinear thresholds rather than smooth linear increments: moderate vertical observation conditions and moderate visual complexity are associated with stronger capitalization, whereas excessive visual competition from surrounding buildings rapidly erodes marginal returns. Third, landmark visual connectivity corresponds to discrete rent surges once critical visibility levels are reached, indicating that iconic heritage nodes reflect threshold like spillover effects on nearby accommodation prices. Fourth, both the magnitude and internal structure of visual premiums vary sharply across districts, demonstrating that capitalization is spatially differentiated rather than spatially uniform within the historic core. By shifting valuation from planar proximity to room-level depth, this study advances three-dimensional visibility assessment and provides an operational framework for view-corridor protection, height control, and differentiated heritage-area management in high-density historic environments. Previous article in issue Next article in issue",
